WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:20.820
MCDONALD’S EXODUS:&nbsp;

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:31.500
McDonald’s operators have had enough. For decades,&nbsp;
they’ve put up with McDonald’s anti franchisee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.320
policies and agressive corporate politics just to&nbsp;
have a chance at growing a thriving restaurant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:43.800
But, the more time they put in, the more they&nbsp;
realize that this deal is far more favorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:50.700
for McDonalds. The average franchisee owner pulls&nbsp;
in $150,000 per year from each restaurant which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:57.540
sounds great until you realize that you have to&nbsp;
invest $1 to $2.2 million to get your hands on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.960
location. In other words, operators are running a&nbsp;
full restaurant business just to earn a 10% return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.180
on their investment. Instead of putting in all&nbsp;
this effort into running a restaurant, operators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:15.300
could just invest their money into the S&amp;P 500&nbsp;
and earn comparable returns with absolutely no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.660
effort. This is only made worse by the fact that&nbsp;
as a franchisee, you’re not even building up your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:27.360
own brand, you’re building up McDonald’s brand&nbsp;
which is doing better than ever. You know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.880
there’s all this fear about recessions, layoffs,&nbsp;
bank failures, interest rates, and what not? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.700
despite all that turmoil, McDonald’s is&nbsp;
doing better than ever. They’re one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.760
only companies’ who stock is at near all time high&nbsp;
levels giving them a valuation of just under $200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.820
billion. A similar story can be seen with their&nbsp;
net income as well. In the last 12 month period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:57.600
McDonald’s profited just over $6 billion. If&nbsp;
we divide that amongst their 38,000 locations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:05.520
we get an average income per store of $162,000&nbsp;
or even more than the average franchisee. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.680
McDonald’s brand image, it’s not surprising&nbsp;
that their able to charge such a premium, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.680
more franchisees than ever are starting question&nbsp;
whether all of this is even worth it, and for many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:24.720
of them, the answer is no. In 2021, a record 13%&nbsp;
of franchise owned locations changed hands. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.940
perspective, only 6% of Burger King franchise&nbsp;
locations changed hands during the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:36.780
period. If we zoom out a little, it just looks&nbsp;
even worse for McDonalds. Between 2019 and 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:43.380
28% of McDonald’s locations have either been&nbsp;
closed or sold off. And according to operators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:49.500
it’s only getting worse. There’s apparently&nbsp;
a line to get out the door. So, here’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.000
McDonald’s operators are leaving in droves and&nbsp;
the frightening implications for McDonald’s.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:02.520
EXPENSIVE REMODELS:
McDonald’s likes to write off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:08.940
exodus as simply pent up demand from the pandemic,&nbsp;
but in reality, there was a pivotal event 2018:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:16.140
remodels. This marked the biggest remodel in&nbsp;
the company’s history in decades and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:22.860
required. McDonald’s wanted new furniture, new&nbsp;
decor, remodeled counters, exterior redesigns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.380
digital kiosks, and reimagined drive thru lanes.&nbsp;
Even if you only go to McDonald’s on an occasional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:35.460
basis, I’m sure you’ve noticed these changes. And&nbsp;
honestly, they’re pretty nice, but they were also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:40.920
quite a burden for franchise owners. McDonald’s&nbsp;
offered to pay 55% of the renovation expenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.840
for the affected 14,000 US restaurants&nbsp;
which came out to a total of $6 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:53.880
This means that the average cost to renovate&nbsp;
each store was a whopping $779,000, out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:00.960
which operators were responsible for $350,000, and&nbsp;
that’s just the average. For older more outdated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:06.840
stores, the renovation expenses were probably&nbsp;
closer to a million dollars if not over which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:12.840
pushes up the operator contribution to nearly&nbsp;
$500,000. This is made even worse by the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.600
the stores that are most likely to be outdated and&nbsp;
run down are ones that aren’t performing as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.400
In other words, the stores that are performing&nbsp;
the worst would likely have to pay the highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.940
bill. For 40% of operators, this was completely&nbsp;
unfeasible as the remodel expense would put them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.620
in danger of not having their leases renewed&nbsp;
based on McDonald’s own financial requirements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:40.740
though McDonald’s does dispute this number.&nbsp;
For obvious reasons, operators were triggered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.680
In fact, when asked to rate their relationship&nbsp;
with McDonald’s from a scale of 1-5, the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:54.480
operator gave a rating of 1.19, and that was in&nbsp;
April of 2022. This was the 3rd worst rating in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.860
the survey’s history and the 2 times that it&nbsp;
was worse were both during the height of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.920
remodels. All of this tension would lead to the&nbsp;
creation of basically a franchisee union named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:09.960
the National Owners Asociation in 2018 which was&nbsp;
the first of its kind in the company’s history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.600
The association has been trying to advocate on&nbsp;
behalf of operators, but it doesn’t seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.600
they’ve been able to make much tangible progress.&nbsp;
In fact, McDonald’s has not only not backed off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:28.380
but they tried to offload even more of the remodel&nbsp;
expenses onto operators. The most notable example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:34.500
of this was a new technology fee that McDonald’s&nbsp;
implemented in late 2020. Apparently, McDonald’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:41.280
was racking up too much debt, so they decided to&nbsp;
charge operators a $70 million technology fee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.920
Operators would end up suing McDonald’s, but&nbsp;
even this didn’t eliminate the fee, though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:52.920
did reduce by 60%. It seems that McDonald’s has&nbsp;
gotten into a habit of prioritizing end consumers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:58.680
and shareholders over everything else which&nbsp;
isn’t a bad thing in itself. The reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:04.980
it’s bad in McDonald’s case is that they’re doing&nbsp;
it at the cost of operators which is not exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:10.620
a very smart move long term given that 93% of&nbsp;
their locations are operated by independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:16.320
business owners. But, expensive remodels and&nbsp;
annoying technology fees are just the tip of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:23.340
iceberg. The truth is that the entire partnership&nbsp;
between operators and McDonald’s is just a facade.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:28.920
FACADE OF A PARTNERSHIP:&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:35.100
When you think McDonald’s, we all think of&nbsp;
burgers, fries, creepy clowns, and big golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:40.320
arches, but what if I told you that McDonald’s&nbsp;
doesn’t actually make money from any of this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.860
While these are what us consumers associate with&nbsp;
McDonald’s, the reality is that we’re not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:54.360
the real end consumer of McDonald’s. The real end&nbsp;
consumer is actually operators, and if you don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:00.720
believe me, just take it from McDonald’s former&nbsp;
CFO himself. “We are not technically in the food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:07.080
business. We are in the real estate business. The&nbsp;
only reason we sell fifteen-cent hamburgers is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:13.020
because they are the greatest producer of revenue,&nbsp;
from which our tenants can pay us our rent.” This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:17.880
might be confusing because it’s been engrained&nbsp;
in all of our minds that McDonalds is a fast food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:25.260
company. But in reality, McDonald’s figured that&nbsp;
that wasn’t all that lucrative decades ago. I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:31.440
just think about it. Even if you sell billions of&nbsp;
burgers every year, if you’re only make 10 or 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.500
cents on each burger, it’s not gonna be that much&nbsp;
money on a company scale. If you own the land of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.300
tens of thousands of the most desirable locations&nbsp;
in the world and charge your operators massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:48.900
leases though, well, now you’re talking. This is&nbsp;
magnitudes more profitable than selling burgers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:54.720
and it’s evident in McDonald’s market cap. They’re&nbsp;
worth over 4 times more than the 2nd largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:00.960
restaurant chain, Chipotle, and they’re worth&nbsp;
almost 20 times more than Domino’s. This isn’t to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.240
say that McDonald’s is 20 times more popular than&nbsp;
Domino’s or that they sell 20 times more food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:13.860
but it does mean that their business is 20 times&nbsp;
more attractive for investors. While this model is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.900
brilliant for investors, it hasn’t been all&nbsp;
that friendly for operators especially over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:23.280
the past few years because of the real estate&nbsp;
boom. Right at the beginning of the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.800
the Fed cut interest rates to 0% which led to&nbsp;
real estate across the country going absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:34.440
wild. And given that McDonald’s is a real estate&nbsp;
business, I don’t think you’d be surprised to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:41.700
that McDonald’s total assets also went wild. But,&nbsp;
this didn’t exactly sit well with operators. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.440
they were struggling to pay cashiers and burger&nbsp;
flippers minimum wage during one of the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:52.140
hospitality crises ever while McDonald’s&nbsp;
was doing better than ever. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:56.100
you can argue that all of this is in the&nbsp;
fine print and operators should look into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:02.100
all aspects of the franchise before signing and&nbsp;
all of that’s true. But with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:08.400
it’s also natural for people to get mad in such a&nbsp;
situation. When you sign up for a franchise, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:13.740
expect to be partners with the company. You expect&nbsp;
to share in the good times and the bad times. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:19.020
in McDonald’s case it seems like the partnership&nbsp;
is cleverly structured such that all of the risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:24.240
of running a low margin fast food business&nbsp;
lies on the operator. And for many operators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.800
the pandemic was the eye opener they needed&nbsp;
to see the partnership for what it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.200
was. And now that things have finally&nbsp;
stabilized on their side as well, they want out.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.160
UGLY BREAKUP:&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.400
If these one sided policies weren’t a good enough&nbsp;
reason to leave, yet another reason to leave is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:49.800
that modern generations aren’t as eager to get&nbsp;
into the fast food business. Traditionally, many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:55.860
McDonald’s locations are run almost like a family&nbsp;
business. A family might own a few locations in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:01.200
their home town that they’ve pass down generation&nbsp;
after generation, but for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:07.500
the new generation doesn’t want in. It’s not just&nbsp;
that McDonald’s franchisee policies aren’t that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:13.500
great but also that there’s way more opportunities&nbsp;
out there thanks to the internet. If you’re gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.900
go down the partner with a big company approach,&nbsp;
why partner with a fast food company when you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:24.780
partner with a tech company. You can become a&nbsp;
Google AdWords partner or a YouTube partner or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:31.440
an Amazon Affiliate marketer or an FBA seller.&nbsp;
The options are basically endless online. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:37.380
you don’t even need to go down the entrepreneurial&nbsp;
route. In the western world, you don’t have to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:43.080
a business to make a $150,000 per year. There’s&nbsp;
plenty of jobs that pay the same or more without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:48.240
the stress of running a full on restaurant.&nbsp;
So, naturally, despite putting in decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.760
into the business, many families want out. These&nbsp;
families would of course prefer an amicable split,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.760 --> 00:11:00.120
but McDonald’s is making this rather difficult as&nbsp;
well. First of all, they don’t even question about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.580
why you’re leaving which is quite surprising. I&nbsp;
mean, if you’re trying to cancel a simple cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:10.260
phone plan, you’ll run into 4 sales reps asking&nbsp;
you why you’re leaving and trying to convince you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:16.860
to stay. But, at McDonalds, not a single persons&nbsp;
asks operators why they’re leaving which simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:23.100
strengthens the idea that they don’t really care&nbsp;
about their operators. That’s quite a shame but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:27.900
the bigger disappointment for many operators is&nbsp;
that they don’t even get to choose who they hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:34.920
off their location to. If you spend 30, 40, 50&nbsp;
years building up a location, it would make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.180
that you would want to hand off the location&nbsp;
to someone you know and trust, but McDonald’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:46.560
is often blocking outright. You see, on paper&nbsp;
they have the right to first refusal meaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:51.960
Traditionally, this wasn’t enforced very strictly,&nbsp;
but given that many operators are starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:56.220
take a stand against McDonald’s, McDonald’s&nbsp;
has resorted to leveraging this right more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:02.940
aggressively. This way, they can route the stores&nbsp;
to more passive investor operators but that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:08.760
makes the situation worse for all of remaining&nbsp;
everyday operators. All of this is leading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:15.360
operators not even missing the business that&nbsp;
they worked on for decades. And as more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:21.000
operators start feeling this way, the McDonald’s&nbsp;
operator exodus is only gonna get worse.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:26.160
THE FUTURE OF MCDONALD’S:&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:33.240
For franchises, it’s usually the operators that&nbsp;
can make or break the business. McDonald’s has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:38.640
cleverly structured their business to minimize&nbsp;
impacts from unhappy operators, but even this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:44.040
will only shield them so much. At the end of the&nbsp;
day, if operators drop the ball on running these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:49.380
stores, people will have a worse experience&nbsp;
at McDonald’s and they will come less often.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:55.740
Eventually, this would lead to locations becoming&nbsp;
unprofitable meaning that operators won’t be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:02.160
to pay McDonald’s their cash cow lease payments.&nbsp;
And if a location is already performing poorly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:08.100
it’s just gonna be even harder to bring in another&nbsp;
operator and try to turn things around especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:14.400
if that operator isn’t all that happy to be there&nbsp;
either. McDonald’s isn’t quite at that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:20.100
but given that 28% of stores were sold off&nbsp;
or shutdown within past couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:26.580
it won’t take long for things to get that bad if&nbsp;
McDonald’s doesn’t take any action. So, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:30.600
it’s in McDonald’s best interest to get on the&nbsp;
good side of their operators as soon as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:36.300
unless they want to continue facing this mass&nbsp;
exodus and ruin their business in record time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:41.880
but that’s just what I think. Do you like&nbsp;
McDonald’s? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:47.040
drop a like if you think that McDonald’s&nbsp;
operator policies are brilliant but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.240
terrible. And of course, consider checking&nbsp;
